<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     1. PROJECT OVERVIEW
══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<h2><span class="section-num">1</span>Project Overview</h2>

<p>
  <strong>Giraffle</strong> is a self-hosted knowledge editor and distributed multi-agent orchestration
  platform built with Next.js 16, React 19, PostgreSQL, and TypeScript. The core idea is twofold:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>A <strong>block-based knowledge editor</strong> (similar to Notion) with wikilinks, backlinks, and graph navigation — all stored canonically in PostgreSQL.</li>
  <li>A <strong>LangGraph-powered orchestration engine</strong> that coordinates multiple AI coding agents (Claude Code, Aider, etc.) running in terminal sessions on remote machines.</li>
</ul>

<p>
  The LangGraph component — the focus of this assignment — is the "Giraffe Orchestrator": a stateful
  state machine that plans work and dispatches tasks across a registry of AI agents, collecting
  handoff notes between them to preserve context.
</p>

<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Technology</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Framework</td><td>Next.js 16 (App Router)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>UI</td><td>React 19 + Tiptap editor</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Database</td><td>PostgreSQL 16 via Prisma 7</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Auth</td><td>NextAuth (credential-based)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>AI Orchestration</td><td>LangGraph (@langchain/langgraph ^1.2.9)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>LLM Client</td><td>AI SDK — @ai-sdk/openai, generateText</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Agent Communication</td><td>tmux (capture-pane, send-keys) over SSH</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Deployment</td><td>Docker Compose + nginx</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>


<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     2. WHY LANGGRAPH?
══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<h2><span class="section-num">2</span>Why LangGraph?</h2>

<p>
  Coordinating multiple AI agents that must hand work off to each other sequentially — while each
  can succeed, fail, or time out — is a classic stateful workflow problem. A simple chain of
  function calls would not handle:
</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Branching</strong>: what to do when the planner returns an empty plan or invalid JSON?</li>
  <li><strong>Loops</strong>: re-entering the same node (<code>runStep</code>) once per plan step.</li>
  <li><strong>State persistence</strong>: every node sees the full, typed state and can update it atomically.</li>
  <li><strong>Abort / cancellation</strong>: passing an <code>AbortSignal</code> from the HTTP layer down to the graph invocation.</li>
</ul>

<p>
  <strong>LangGraph</strong> provides exactly these primitives: typed state annotations, nodes as
  pure async functions, and conditional edges that decide the next node at runtime. The result is
  a graph that is easy to reason about as a diagram and equally easy to extend.
</p>


<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     3. ARCHITECTURE
══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<h2><span class="section-num">3</span>Architecture: Smart Orchestrator, Dumb Agents</h2>

<p>The architectural philosophy behind the orchestrator is deliberately asymmetric:</p>

<ul>
  <li>
    <strong>Smart Orchestrator</strong> — the LangGraph state machine. It uses an LLM (GPT-4o by default)
    to decompose a high-level goal into an ordered list of steps, deciding which agent handles each step.
    It manages timing, timeouts, handoffs, and persistence.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Dumb Agents</strong> — existing CLI tools (Claude Code, Aider, custom scripts) that run in
    terminal sessions. The orchestrator does <em>not</em> set their model or system prompt; those are
    baked into the CLI tool's own configuration. The orchestrator only types text into the terminal
    and waits for the idle marker (e.g., the shell prompt <code>&gt; </code>).
  </li>
</ul>

<p>
  This separation means any terminal-based AI coding tool can be a "dumb agent" without modification.
  New agent types can be registered through the UI by specifying their start command and idle marker.
</p>

<div class="diagram">
  <span class="node-box oval">User / HTTP Request</span>
  <br>
  <span class="arrow">↓</span>
  <br>
  <span class="node-box highlight">Giraffe Orchestrator<br>(LangGraph State Machine)</span>
  <br>
  <span class="arrow">↓</span>
  <br>
  <span style="display:inline-flex; gap:24px; align-items:center; flex-wrap:wrap; justify-content:center;">
    <span class="node-box">Agent A<br>(Claude Code)</span>
    <span class="node-box">Agent B<br>(Aider)</span>
    <span class="node-box">Agent N<br>(Custom CLI)</span>
  </span>
  <br>
  <span style="font-size:9pt; color:var(--text-muted);">← SSH / tmux sessions on remote machines →</span>
</div>


<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     4. LANGGRAPH STATE MACHINE — DEEP DIVE
══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<h2><span class="section-num">4</span>LangGraph State Machine — Implementation Deep Dive</h2>

<p>The full source lives at <code>src/lib/orchestrator.ts</code>. The graph has two nodes:</p>

<div class="diagram">
  <span class="node-box oval highlight">START</span>
  <span class="arrow"> → </span>
  <span class="node-box">planner</span>
  <span class="arrow"> → </span>
  <br><br>
  <span class="condition-label">if plan.length &gt; 0 &amp;&amp; phase ≠ "failed"</span><br>
  <span class="arrow">↓</span>
  <br>
  <span class="node-box">runStep</span>
  <span class="arrow"> ⟲ </span>
  <span style="font-size: 9pt; color: var(--text-muted);">(loops until all steps done or failure)</span>
  <br><br>
  <span class="condition-label">if phase = "done" | "failed" | aborted</span><br>
  <span class="arrow">↓</span>
  <br>
  <span class="node-box oval highlight">END</span>
</div>

<p>
  The graph allows up to <strong>64 recursive steps</strong> (the <code>recursionLimit</code>) which
  supports sessions with up to ~30 agent tasks before a hard stop.
</p>


<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     5. STATE SCHEMA
══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<h2><span class="section-num">5</span>State Schema</h2>

<p>
  LangGraph uses <code>Annotation.Root</code> to define typed state. Each field declares a
  <em>reducer</em> (how new values are merged) and a default. Fields without an explicit reducer
  replace the previous value.
</p>

<pre>import { StateGraph, Annotation, END, START } from "@langchain/langgraph";

const OrchestratorState = Annotation.Root({
  // Session identity
  sessionId:        Annotation&lt;string&gt;(),
  goal:             Annotation&lt;string&gt;(),
  workingDirectory: Annotation&lt;string&gt;(),
  supervisorModel:  Annotation&lt;string&gt;(),

  // Agent registry — replace-on-write
  agentRegistry: Annotation&lt;AgentInfo[]&gt;({
    reducer: (_, y) =&gt; y,
    default: () =&gt; [],
  }),

  // Plan produced by the planner node — replace-on-write
  plan: Annotation&lt;PlanStep[]&gt;({
    reducer: (_, y) =&gt; y,
    default: () =&gt; [],
  }),

  currentStepIndex: Annotation&lt;number&gt;({ reducer: (_, y) =&gt; y, default: () =&gt; 0 }),
  handoffPayload:   Annotation&lt;string&gt;({ reducer: (_, y) =&gt; y, default: () =&gt; "" }),
  phase:            Annotation&lt;string&gt;({ reducer: (_, y) =&gt; y, default: () =&gt; "planning" }),
  aborted:          Annotation&lt;boolean&gt;({ reducer: (_, y) =&gt; y, default: () =&gt; false }),
  error:            Annotation&lt;string&gt;({ reducer: (_, y) =&gt; y, default: () =&gt; "" }),
});</pre>

<p>Each <code>PlanStep</code> carries:</p>

<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Type</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>agentId</code></td><td>string</td><td>Database ID of the assigned agent</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>agentLabel</code></td><td>string</td><td>Human-readable agent name</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>task</code></td><td>string</td><td>Natural language task description</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>status</code></td><td>pending | executing | done | failed</td><td>Live progress tracking</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>handoffNote</code></td><td>string</td><td>Context note written by the agent for the next one</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>


<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     6. GRAPH NODES
══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<h2><span class="section-num">6</span>Graph Nodes</h2>

<h3>6.1 — <code>planner</code> Node</h3>

<p>
  The planner node calls an OpenAI model with the session goal and the list of available agents.
  It asks the model to return a raw JSON array of steps — no markdown fences allowed. The response
  is cleaned and parsed; on failure the phase is set to <code>"failed"</code> and the graph ends
  immediately via conditional routing.
</p>

<pre>async function plannerNode(state: OState): Promise&lt;Partial&lt;OState&gt;&gt; {
  const { text } = await generateText({
    model: openai(supervisorModel || "gpt-4o"),
    system: `You are an orchestrator that plans tasks for AI coding agents in terminals.`,
    prompt: `Goal: ${goal}\nAvailable agents:\n${agentDescriptions}\n
Return a JSON array. Each step:
{ "agentId": "&lt;id&gt;", "agentLabel": "&lt;label&gt;", "task": "&lt;description&gt;" }
Start with [ and end with ].`,
    temperature: 0.1,
  });

  const plan = JSON.parse(text.trim()).map(step =&gt; ({
    ...step, status: "pending", handoffNote: ""
  }));

  await updateAgentSessionPlan(sessionId, plan);
  return { plan, currentStepIndex: 0, phase: "executing" };
}</pre>

<h3>6.2 — <code>runStep</code> Node</h3>

<p>The most complex node. For each step it:</p>

<ol>
  <li>Looks up the target agent in <code>agentRegistry</code>.</li>
  <li>Prepends the previous agent's handoff note to the task message (if any).</li>
  <li>Writes the task to the agent's tmux terminal via <code>sendToAgentInput()</code>.</li>
  <li>Polls <code>waitForIdleMarker()</code> — checking that the agent's configured idle marker appears in terminal output — with a 5-minute timeout.</li>
  <li>Captures the terminal output via <code>captureTmuxPane()</code> and persists it as an <code>AgentMessage</code>.</li>
  <li>If more steps remain, asks the current agent to write a <code>[HANDOFF_START]…[HANDOFF_END]</code> note, waits for idle again, and extracts the note.</li>
  <li>Marks the step as <code>"done"</code> and advances <code>currentStepIndex</code>.</li>
</ol>

<pre>async function runStepNode(state: OState): Promise&lt;Partial&lt;OState&gt;&gt; {
  const { sendToAgentInput, waitForIdleMarker,
          extractHandoffNote, captureTmuxPane } =
    await import("@/lib/ws-terminal-server");

  // 1. Send task (with handoff from previous agent if any)
  const taskMessage = handoffPayload
    ? `Previous agent's handoff:\n${handoffPayload}\n\n---\nYour task: ${step.task}`
    : step.task;
  await sendToAgentInput(agent.id, taskMessage);

  // 2. Wait for idle (5-minute timeout)
  const isIdle = await waitForIdleMarker(agent.id, agent.idleMarker, 300_000);
  if (!isIdle) return { phase: "failed", error: "Agent timed out" };

  // 3. Capture output
  const agentOutput = await captureTmuxPane(agent.id, 100);

  // 4. Request handoff note if more steps remain
  if (!isLastStep) {
    await sendToAgentInput(agent.id, handoffPrompt);
    await waitForIdleMarker(agent.id, agent.idleMarker, 120_000);
    newHandoffPayload = await extractHandoffNote(agent.id);
  }

  return { plan: updatedPlan, currentStepIndex: nextIndex, handoffPayload: newHandoffPayload };
}</pre>


<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     7. CONDITIONAL ROUTING
══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<h2><span class="section-num">7</span>Conditional Routing (Edges)</h2>

<p>
  LangGraph's conditional edges call a <em>router function</em> after each node completes.
  The function returns either the name of the next node or <code>END</code>.
</p>

<pre>// After planner: go to runStep unless planning failed
function routeAfterPlanner(state: OState): "runStep" | typeof END {
  if (state.phase === "failed" || state.plan.length === 0) return END;
  return "runStep";
}

// After each step: loop back unless session is done/failed/aborted
function routeAfterStep(state: OState): "runStep" | typeof END {
  if (state.phase === "done" || state.phase === "failed" || state.aborted) return END;
  return "runStep";
}</pre>

<p>
  This implements a <strong>loop without explicit loop constructs</strong> — a key LangGraph pattern.
  The node runs, updates state, then the router decides whether to re-enter the same node or end.
</p>


<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     8. GRAPH ASSEMBLY
══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<h2><span class="section-num">8</span>Graph Assembly &amp; Compilation</h2>

<pre>const graph = new StateGraph(OrchestratorState)
  .addNode("planner", plannerNode)
  .addNode("runStep", runStepNode)
  .addEdge(START, "planner")                         // always enter planner first
  .addConditionalEdges("planner", routeAfterPlanner) // plan ok? → runStep, else END
  .addConditionalEdges("runStep", routeAfterStep);   // more steps? → runStep, else END

const orchestratorGraph = graph.compile();</pre>

<p>
  <code>graph.compile()</code> validates the graph topology (no unreachable nodes, all edges defined)
  and returns an executable runnable. The graph is invoked with:
</p>

<pre>const finalState = await orchestratorGraph.invoke(initialState, {
  recursionLimit: 64,  // max node invocations before automatic stop
  signal,              // AbortSignal from HTTP request for cancellation
});</pre>


<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     9. DATABASE SCHEMA
══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<h2><span class="section-num">9</span>Database Schema for Agents</h2>

<p>
  Five Prisma models back the agent orchestration system. All IDs are UUIDs; timestamps are managed
  by Prisma.
</p>

<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Key Fields</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>Machine</code></td>
      <td>A remote server reachable over SSH</td>
      <td>host, port, username, authType, sshCredential (AES-256 encrypted), status</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>Agent</code></td>
      <td>An AI CLI tool running on a machine</td>
      <td>machineId, agentType, agentCommand, idleMarker, status</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>AgentSession</code></td>
      <td>One orchestration run (goal → plan → execution)</td>
      <td>goal, supervisorModel, workingDirectory, plan (JSON), status, endedAt</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>AgentSessionAgent</code></td>
      <td>Many-to-many: which agents participate in a session</td>
      <td>sessionId, agentId</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>AgentMessage</code></td>
      <td>Full message log of the orchestration session</td>
      <td>sessionId, fromAgentId?, toAgentId?, role, content, messageType</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>
  The <code>plan</code> column on <code>AgentSession</code> is a <code>Json</code> field that stores
  the live <code>PlanStep[]</code> array. It is updated after every state transition so the UI can
  poll and display real-time progress.
</p>


<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     10. API SURFACE
══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<h2><span class="section-num">10</span>API Surface</h2>

<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Route</th><th>Method</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>/api/agents/sessions/[id]/start</code></td><td>POST</td><td>Invokes <code>runOrchestrator()</code>; the graph runs in the background. Returns 202 immediately.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>/api/agents/sessions/[id]/state</code></td><td>GET</td><td>Polls live session state (plan, messages, status) for UI updates.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>/api/agents/sessions</code></td><td>POST</td><td>Creates a new AgentSession record and links selected agents.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>/api/agents</code></td><td>GET / POST</td><td>CRUD for agent definitions.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>/api/agents/machines</code></td><td>GET / POST</td><td>CRUD for machine registrations (SSH credentials stored encrypted).</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>


<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     11. FULL-STACK INTEGRATION
══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<h2><span class="section-num">11</span>Full-Stack Integration (Next.js App Router)</h2>

<p>The orchestrator is wired end-to-end through Next.js 16's App Router:</p>

<ol>
  <li>
    <strong>Server Components</strong> (<code>src/app/(main)/agents/page.tsx</code>) fetch machines,
    agents, and sessions in parallel with <code>Promise.all</code>, then pass serialized data to a client component.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Client Component</strong> (<code>AgentsHubClient</code>) renders the UI: machine list,
    agent registry, session creation form, and live session detail view with step-by-step progress.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Session detail page</strong> (<code>src/app/(main)/agents/sessions/[id]/page.tsx</code>)
    polls <code>/api/agents/sessions/[id]/state</code> to show real-time plan execution status,
    message log, and per-step handoff notes.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>WebSocket terminal</strong> (<code>src/lib/ws-terminal-server.ts</code>) manages active
    tmux sessions per agent, providing <code>sendToAgentInput</code>, <code>waitForIdleMarker</code>,
    and <code>captureTmuxPane</code> used by <code>runStepNode</code>.
  </li>
</ol>

