<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     15. REFLECTION & DISCUSSION POINTS
══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<h2><span class="section-num">15</span>Reflection &amp; Discussion Points</h2>

<h3>What LangGraph enables that a plain function chain cannot</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Typed, inspectable state</strong>: every field has a declared reducer and a default. Debugging is easier because state is explicit.</li>
  <li><strong>Runtime branching</strong>: the router function reads live state, so decisions like "abort if timed out" require no special framework plumbing.</li>
  <li><strong>Natural loops</strong>: the <code>runStep → runStep</code> self-loop with a terminal condition is a first-class graph pattern, not a workaround.</li>
  <li><strong>Cancellation</strong>: passing an <code>AbortSignal</code> into <code>graph.invoke()</code> propagates cancellation automatically across all async node invocations.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Design decisions and trade-offs</h3>
<ul>
  <li>
    <strong>Replace-on-write reducers</strong> for <code>plan</code> and <code>agentRegistry</code>:
    these are always written as complete arrays, so a simple last-write-wins reducer is correct and
    prevents accidental partial merges.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Polling vs. streaming</strong>: the orchestrator polls the terminal for an idle marker
    rather than streaming token output. This is simpler, works with any CLI tool, and avoids
    WebSocket complexity inside the graph.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Out-of-band persistence</strong>: the graph calls <code>updateAgentSessionPlan()</code>
    directly inside nodes rather than using LangGraph checkpointers. This keeps the PostgreSQL schema
    fully under the application's control.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Temperature 0.1 for the planner</strong>: low temperature makes the JSON output more
    deterministic and reduces parse failures.
  </li>
</ul>

<h3>Possible extensions</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Add a <strong>validator node</strong> after <code>runStep</code> that checks test results before advancing.</li>
  <li>Use <strong>LangGraph checkpointers</strong> (PostgreSQL adapter) to persist graph state between server restarts.</li>
  <li>Support <strong>parallel step execution</strong> using LangGraph's <code>Send</code> API when steps have no dependencies.</li>
  <li>Add a <strong>human-in-the-loop</strong> pause using LangGraph's interruption / approval mechanism.</li>
</ul>

<div class="info-box" style="margin-top: 48px;">
  <strong>Summary</strong>
  Giraffle demonstrates LangGraph's core strengths — typed state, conditional routing, and node
  loops — in a real production context: coordinating multiple AI coding agents across remote machines.
  The graph is small (two nodes) but the pattern it implements — plan then iteratively execute —
  is directly applicable to any multi-agent workflow.
</div>

